ROAST.PET WEIRD BUILDER PROJECTS =E2=80=94 2026-09-20 02:24 Asia/Phnom_Penh

Screened 25+ plausible projects/results across GitHub, Reddit
technical communities, pet CV/landmarks, browser rendering,
transparent video, identity QA, WebAR and POD tooling. Six survived
after excluding prior-run discoveries.

1) Personalab =E2=80=94 simonamador/personalab
URL: https://github.com/simonamador/personalab
Activity: 2 commits in current indexed repo; current project surfaced Sep 2=
026.
License: MIT.
Solves: the missing identity-QA orchestration layer. It evaluates
every generated identity against an anchor, separates identity from
visual quality, logs drift, and drives deterministic
ACCEPT/RETRY/REJECT decisions. It already has provider adapters,
concurrency/rate limiting, prompt-patch retries and per-frame video
identity evaluation.
Install: Python >=3D3.10; pip install -e .; optional InsightFace/ONNX
evaluation extras.
Dependencies/hardware: numpy/OpenCV/Pydantic; optional InsightFace +
ONNX Runtime; evaluation is CPU-capable (~200 ms/image reported).
Generation uses Gemini/OpenAI/Runware/Replicate/Runway adapters.
Evidence: repo reports measured same-person ArcFace similarity
0.49=E2=80=930.75, ~200 ms identity evaluation, 278 unit tests, video tempo=
ral
identity metrics, explicit retry/drift logs.
Roast.pet value: do NOT use its human ArcFace scorer directly for
pets. Reuse the architecture and replace IdentityEvaluator with an
animal embedding + pet landmark scorer. This could replace most of our
planned generation retry controller, QA metrics logging, provider
routing and longitudinal identity-drift code.
Difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM; architecture is generic, current repo is
very young.
Experiment: fork the evaluator interface; implement
PetIdentityEvaluator with (a) pet embedding similarity, (b)
eye/nose/mouth/ear geometry, (c) coat-color histogram/local patches.
Run 100 accepted/rejected generated pet states and test whether
composite score predicts human PASS/FAIL.
Verdict: FORK / COPY ARCHITECTURE.

2) snapml-quadruped-pose =E2=80=94 EstevanSL / Sausage Labs
URL: https://github.com/EstevanSL/snapml-quadruped-pose
Activity: 33 commits; publicly shared Aug/Sep 2026 and verified in
Lens Studio 5.23.1.
License: Apache-2.0 code + pose weights; AP-10K annotations CC BY 4.0;
bundled Snap detector has separate Snap terms. Upstream AP-10K
commercial-license wording has a documented discrepancy, so confirm
before commercial shipping.
Solves: tiny real-time 17-joint animal pose tracking across 54
quadruped species. 3.26M parameters, 3.36 MB shipped.
Install/demo: import AnimalTracker.lspkg into Lens Studio; for ML path
export/run ONNX model. Repo includes deterministic conformance test
against ONNX Runtime.
Dependencies/hardware: Lens Studio for Lens runtime; Python 3.10/3.11
+ CUDA for training. Inference is designed on-device.
Evidence: measured 0.6289 AP on AP-10K val; 0.4324 at 64 px subject
detail; DARK subpixel decode + One Euro smoothing; verified Lens
Studio preview.
Roast.pet value: this is far smaller than the ViTPose/AP10K stacks
previously found and gives a concrete recipe for cheap on-device pet
pose. Useful for reaction-pose extraction, future live pet AR, and
motion QA.
Difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM due to Snap detector/license boundary; pose
code itself is cleanly isolated.
Experiment: run 20 dog/cat clips through its ONNX pose model outside
Lens Studio, export 17-point tracks, and compare pose stability/CPU
latency with the earlier ViTPose route. If adequate, use it as the
cheap pose extractor.
Verdict: BENCHMARK NOW / FORK MODEL PIPELINE.

3) rt-alpha-video =E2=80=94 Rethink-JS/alpha-video-cli
URL: https://github.com/Rethink-JS/alpha-video-cli
Activity: current v1.2.0; 1 star, 0 forks. Companion web helper had 10
commits and was updated Dec 3 2025.
License: MIT.
Solves: transparent pet animation delivery. Folder of RGBA PNG frames
-> VP9-alpha WebM + HEVC-alpha MOV + ready-to-paste runtime HTML that
chooses the correct format for Safari/iOS vs other browsers. It also
verifies alpha, detects frame naming patterns and prints compression
stats.
Install: npx @rethink-js/alpha-video-cli --input ./frames --fps 50.
Dependencies/hardware: Node >=3D16 + FFmpeg; HEVC-alpha path is
macOS/VideoToolbox specific.
Evidence: stable 1.0 then 1.1/1.2 changelog; browser helper previews
sequence and verifies transparency before encoding.
Roast.pet value: we should not ship PNG sequences for animated
transparent pets and should not hand-write Safari/WebM fallback logic.
Difficulty: LOW.
Maintenance risk: LOW; tiny FFmpeg wrapper.
Experiment: encode one 5-second approved transparent pet loop from PNG
frames. Compare PNG-sequence bytes/load time vs WebM+HEVC and test
iPhone Safari + Android Chrome.
Verdict: REUSE NOW as packaging CLI.

4) VidAPI =E2=80=94 moshehbenavraham/vidapi
URL: https://github.com/moshehbenavraham/vidapi
Activity: current indexed repo, 6 stars / 2 forks; active
documentation covers current production stack.
License: MIT.
Solves: if Roast.pet needs a render service, almost all boring server
plumbing already exists: JSON composition schema, FastAPI, async
queue, Redis, SQLite/Postgres, S3/MinIO, webhooks, reusable templates,
captions, posters, TikTok/Reels/Shorts presets, FFmpeg/Editly/HTML
render adapters.
Install: docker compose up --build; smoke-test script included.
Dependencies/hardware: Docker;
FastAPI/ARQ/Redis/FFmpeg/Editly/HyperFrames; no special GPU needed for
deterministic composition.
Evidence: documented end-to-end smoke test, validated Pydantic
composition schema, render artifacts, sidecar/burn-in captions, poster
metadata, production storage path.
Roast.pet value: if Kamishibai/LeClap stays as renderer, VidAPI can be
the job-service shell around it. Do not build render
queues/webhooks/storage/status APIs ourselves.
Difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM; larger dependency surface than our V0 needs.
Experiment: add a single Roast renderer adapter that accepts
pet-package + performance.json, render 20 concurrent fake orders,
kill/restart a worker mid-batch and verify artifacts/status recover
correctly.
Verdict: COPY SERVICE SHELL / WATCH; don't adopt until asynchronous
server rendering is actually needed.

5) Frederic =E2=80=94 zylamarek/frederic
URL: https://github.com/zylamarek/frederic
Activity: 53 commits; older project, not a current frontier model.
License: MIT.
Solves: tiny cat-face geometry: ROI plus five landmarks =E2=80=94 both eyes=
,
mouth, both ears =E2=80=94 using MobileNetV2-based models.
Install: clone + Keras/Pillow; supplied install scripts/conda
environment; pretrained models download on first prediction.
Dependencies/hardware: Keras; CPU possible, GPU optional.
Evidence: author reports average landmark-axis error 2.07 px / 1.6% of
cat-face size and explains training/augmentation/model architecture.
Roast.pet value: the exact five points are sufficient for
deterministic mouth placement, eye overlays, head scale/roll and
ear-relative geometry. Old model quality may be enough because our
source image is a single clean customer portrait.
Difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: HIGH as a direct dependency because Keras stack is
old; LOW as a geometry baseline.
Experiment: 50 customer-style cat photos; measure mouth-center/eye/ear
errors and compare with VLM/manual landmarks. If adequate,
export/modernize to ONNX rather than preserving old environment.
Verdict: COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK, not production dependency.

6) PetFace / PetFaceClone =E2=80=94 animal identity scorer research baselin=
e
URL: https://github.com/SciShaman/PetFaceClone
Project: https://dahlian00.github.io/PetFacePage/
Activity: official code released Jul 2024; face alignment added Oct 7
2024; 21 commits in surfaced clone.
License/usage: IMPORTANT =E2=80=94 dataset, code and pretrained models are
explicitly NON-COMMERCIAL RESEARCH ONLY. Do not ship them in
Roast.pet.
Solves: proves animal identity embeddings are practical. PetFace
covers 257,484 individual animals across 13 families/319 breeds;
ArcFace was the strongest benchmark. Code includes cat
verification/re-ID, pretrained weights and face alignment from pet
keypoints.
Dependencies/hardware: PyTorch/CUDA stack for training/evaluation.
Evidence: ECCV oral work; verification/re-identification evaluation
scripts; PetFace reports jointly trained animal model separates
same/different identities better than generic CLIP/MegaDescriptor
baselines.
Roast.pet value: this answers the conceptual question behind
Personalab adaptation: identity embeddings can work on pets. But
commercial restrictions mean use only as a research benchmark/design
reference, then source/train a commercially usable scorer.
Difficulty: HIGH if retraining a commercial model; LOW for research benchma=
rk.
Maintenance risk: HIGH for production due to license restriction.
Experiment: research-only offline test on 20 pets with multiple real
photos + generated states. Compare PetFace embedding similarity
against CLIP/DINO and human identity judgments. Use results only to
decide what kind of commercial scorer to build/source.
Verdict: WATCH / RESEARCH ONLY / DO NOT SHIP.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES
1. Identity QA orchestration, retry policy, drift logs and provider
throttling =E2=80=94 adapt Personalab's architecture.
2. A heavyweight quadruped pose stack before testing the 3.36 MB
SnapML/AP-10K model.
3. Transparent-animation browser codec handling =E2=80=94 rt-alpha-video
already packages WebM + Safari HEVC fallback.
4. Render-job queues/webhooks/artifact storage if/when we need a
render service =E2=80=94 VidAPI already supplies the shell.
5. Pet identity scoring from first principles =E2=80=94 existing PetFace
research proves the embedding approach; source a commercially usable
equivalent rather than inventing the metric blindly.

MOST SURPRISING HOBBY PROJECT
snapml-quadruped-pose. It was posted to r/Spectacles in Aug/Sep 2026
as a community project, yet ships a 3.36 MB, 3.26M-parameter 17-joint
animal tracker with measured AP, training scripts, ONNX export, a Lens
prefab and deterministic conformance testing. This is exactly the
tiny-specialist-project profile the scanner is targeting.

FASTEST INTEGRATION WIN
rt-alpha-video. Feed one existing transparent pet PNG sequence to a
one-line npx command and test the generated WebM/HEVC pair on iPhone
Safari and Android Chrome. If it works, transparent pet delivery is
solved rather than becoming another Roast.pet subsystem.

ONE REPO/AUTHOR WORTH FOLLOWING
EstevanSL / Sausage Labs. Their stated focus is computer vision for
dogs, and they released quadruped pose infrastructure because they
needed it internally. That is unusually aligned with Roast.pet's
future need for cheap pet-specific geometry and tracking.

ARCHITECTURE UPDATE
The strongest combination from this run is Personalab's generic
IdentityEvaluator protocol + a future commercially usable pet
embedding + cheap pet landmarks. That gives Roast.pet a real identity
firewall:

canonical pet -> generate candidate state -> pet embedding similarity
+ landmark geometry + visual-quality checks -> ACCEPT / targeted RETRY
/ REJECT -> immutable pet package.

That is stronger than generic vision-model QA because identity becomes
measurable, logged and comparable across providers over time.
